    {
      "abstract": "Abstract To address the key bottlenecks of high cost and large seabed occupation in traditional catenary mooring systems for deep‑water floating offshore wind turbines, this paper proposes a lightweight mooring system optimization method based on the synergy of clump weights and buoys. Using a 20 MW semi-submersible floating wind turbine as the research object, the study first compares the influence of three inter-chain angles (15°, 30°, and 45°) on maximum mooring tension under rated wind, wave, and current conditions. The results show that the 15° configuration performs best under all critical incident angles, achieving a maximum tension of 1297.41 kN at 0° incidence. To further improve the load condition of the mooring system, buoys are introduced at the middle‑lower part of the catenary line, and the effect of different placement positions on the peak tension is investigated. Experimental data indicate that the optimal buoy arrangement reduces the maximum tension by 26.5%. This study provides a design reference that balances safety and economy for deep‑water floating wind turbine mooring systems.",
