    {
      "abstract": "The design optimization of floating wind turbine (FWT) substructures involves strong nonlinear couplings among aerodynamic, hydrodynamic, and structural responses, leading to high computational demands and limited design efficiency. To address these challenges, this study proposes an artificial intelligence (AI) enhanced time-domain integrated design optimization framework built on the in-house Pywind platform (Pywind-IDO). The framework integrates automated parametric modeling with a hybrid AI optimization strategy, combining Latin Hypercube Sampling (LHS) for global exploration and Bayesian Optimization (BO) for adaptive learning and local refinement within a unified computational workflow. A case study was investigated to evaluate the effects of sampling and learning strategies in both reduced and full parameter spaces. Results show that the hybrid approach achieves approximately 30%-40% higher mean optimal reward compared with the pure LHS sampling and random-sampling BO baselines, while also improving convergence stability. Correlation and principal component analyses further indicate that top-column radius and down-column distance dominate the optimization landscape, confirming both efficiency and physical interpretability. The proposed framework establishes a reproducible, data-efficient, and intelligent pathway for preliminary design of FWT substructures, paving the way for future multi-objective and uncertainty-aware design under dynamic marine environments.",
